1
00:00:00,243 --> 00:00:02,853
Why does any of this matter in practice? Because

2
00:00:02,853 --> 00:00:05,413
a language model has a fixed number of positions

3
00:00:05,413 --> 00:00:07,653
it can look at. That limit is counted in

4
00:00:07,653 --> 00:00:10,611
tokens, not in words, and not in characters.

5
00:00:10,885 --> 00:00:14,785
Picture a context of twelve positions. A finely split

6
00:00:14,785 --> 00:00:17,745
version of a sentence fills eleven of them. Only

7
00:00:17,745 --> 00:00:19,331
one position is left.

8
00:00:19,470 --> 00:00:24,158
The same sentence, tokenized with larger pieces, needs six.

9
00:00:24,158 --> 00:00:27,860
Same text, twice the room to spare. These particular

10
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:30,160
counts are made up to make the point, but

11
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,671
the effect is real.

12
00:00:31,897 --> 00:00:35,687
So the tokenizer decides how much text fits, and

13
00:00:35,687 --> 00:00:38,325
how much work the model does to read it.

14
00:00:38,325 --> 00:00:41,707
One caution though. A lower token count does not

15
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:45,767
by itself make a model better. Architecture, training, and

16
00:00:45,767 --> 00:00:48,598
the context limit all matter just as much.

17
00:00:49,263 --> 00:00:52,413
Before we finish, a question. We ran the same

18
00:00:52,413 --> 00:00:55,113
string through four trained tokenizers, and they did not

19
00:00:55,113 --> 00:00:57,638
agree. Which statement does that support?

20
00:00:57,855 --> 00:01:02,865
One: every tokenizer produces the same sequence. Two: every

21
00:01:02,865 --> 00:01:08,235
character becomes exactly one token. Three: different trained tokenizers

22
00:01:08,235 --> 00:01:11,845
can segment the same input differently, and can preserve

23
00:01:11,845 --> 00:01:13,364
or lose parts of it.

24
00:01:13,589 --> 00:01:16,719
The answer is three. BERT lowercased the word before

25
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:20,752
splitting it. GPT-2 recovered the emoji from raw bytes,

26
00:01:20,752 --> 00:01:24,459
and BERT could not. And GPT-4 used two pieces

27
00:01:24,459 --> 00:01:29,624
where FLAN-T5 used five. Same input, different outcomes, every time.

28
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,130
So let us put the journey back together. Raw

29
00:01:33,130 --> 00:01:36,870
text is segmented into tokens. Each token is looked

30
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:40,544
up in the vocabulary and becomes an integer ID.

31
00:01:40,544 --> 00:01:43,591
And that sequence of IDs is what the model receives.

32
00:01:43,869 --> 00:01:46,489
Those IDs now move inside the model, where a

33
00:01:46,489 --> 00:01:49,969
second table is waiting. Each ID selects one row

34
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:52,199
of that table, and the row is a learned

35
00:01:52,199 --> 00:01:55,978
vector. That vector is what the model actually computes with.

36
00:01:57,532 --> 00:02:00,612
Keep the distinction clear. An ID is a discrete

37
00:02:00,612 --> 00:02:04,092
label. It has no size and no direction. The

38
00:02:04,092 --> 00:02:06,652
meaning lives in the vector it selects, not in

39
00:02:06,652 --> 00:02:07,918
the number itself.

40
00:02:08,206 --> 00:02:11,346
And one consequence that is easy to miss. A

41
00:02:11,346 --> 00:02:14,556
pretrained language model is tied to the tokenizer it

42
00:02:14,556 --> 00:02:17,226
was trained with. It cannot simply swap in a

43
00:02:17,226 --> 00:02:20,376
different one without being trained again. Which leaves our

44
00:02:20,376 --> 00:02:24,151
next question: how can a list of numbers represent meaning?
